e any ideas please let me  know okay this is the easy one rich and  lonely or poor and happy come on guys  obviously rich and lonely  just kidding  hello darkness smile poor and happy yeah  okay next question is a very interesting  question  as a non-architect what are the things i  should know so that i'm not taken  advantage by an architect i guess this  question depends on  whose point of view you're asking from  you could be asking from a client's  point of view or an engineer's point of  view or an interior designer  or a product rep from a client's  perspective you're right there's  probably times where it's possible that  you could feel taken advantage of  and probably one of the scenarios could  be  you were convinced to spend more money  in this particular area there are  probably a few things that architects  have expertise in  that gives them power over other people  which is the building code  in a lot of cases you kind of need to  take the architect's word for it  and unfortunately there's very few ways  you can kind of check for yourself  whether  or not they're telling the truth well  they're bound by the law to tell the  truth but i mean the code is very  cryptic  you won't be able to really  look into it yourself i've seen  situations because i worked at an  interior design firm before where the  interior designers would hire an outside  architect to go over the code and stamp  the drawings i remember there were  certain design things we wanted to do  that would require the architect to go  kind of above and beyond the code  standards and he  basically just told us that  it wasn't possible based on my  experience with the city i  like having read and studied the code  knew that it was a possibility but  the architect just didn't want to spend  the time to argue with the city about it  because i had very little experience and  this outside architect had lots of  experience we followed his way  so  code that's the one thing the architect  could also try to  trick you with function architects  sometimes use function as an excuse to  introduce a formal or aesthetic element  to the project  that may end up costing you more money  for example the architect might  introduce polish concrete as the most  durable and long-lasting flooring option  but there's also  other very durable materials that don't  cost as much they could also  like introduce a really  intricate ceiling panel system for your  entire ceiling for acoustic absorption  but  um it might just be because they want to  get that look all that being said most  architects just want what's best for you  because they  live in constant fear of their clients  not liking something that they design  even if you think they're trying to take  advantage of you it's for your own good  and it's kind of like when your parents  trick you into eating your vegetable  by mixing it into  a sugary cereal  yeah no that sounds terrible  like when they mix it in a meatball  pasta that's probably a better  comparison since getting fully qualified  has your role change for the better or  worse in the uk architects have a more  project manager role and get to do less  design work which i see as a downside so  far i haven't seen any downsides to  getting licensed in terms of the work  that i'm doing at my firm i'm managing  my own projects and yeah i do have to do  more admin and coordination work it's  true  less time is spent on pure design or  concept but i think that's just the  natural result of managing your own  project and for me uh right now i just  want to know everything that's going on  in my project because that's how you can  kind of turn the design into real life i  think a lot of people have really great  designs but where it goes wrong is kind  of a lot of the times in the execution  so i want to know like exactly where the  sprinklers are going and where all the  ducts are going what level of sheen  we're using for the floors you kind of  do that by being involved 