ike  similar tasks together so that i'm not  switching from doing emails to doing  drawings to doing phone calls like when  i have a bunch of phone calls to make  then i just make that all into a list  and i do all the phone calls all at the  same time switching back and forth and  this has been documented  switching back and forth is  a huge waste of time and it takes out a  lot of energy out of you and it also  takes a lot of time for you to get back  to what you're doing so yeah i try to  schedule similar tasks into the same  chunk of the day and every morning like  i said i try to spend around 15 minutes  to just like try to figure out what i'm  gonna do that day as things come  throughout the week things  shift  from the schedule that i'd made and  that's like totally fine  generally give myself like  a time limit for doing certain tasks so  if i need to finish doing floor plans  i'll give myself like two hours usually  like my days never go as i planned it  but  having that time limit and the schedule  just helps me know where i am compared  to my schedule  and like what i should be reaching for  how are you earning money during  architecture school to cut costs on the  buying of materials printing and all  since there's less time to spare for  ourselves when i was in school in the  first four years i wasn't working but i  worked as a ta and ra when i was in my  master's basically i think the thing  that made the biggest difference for me  was that like actually checked out the  cost of tuition for all the schools that  i was applying for for my master's my  decision to go back to the school that i  went for my bachelor's was like  primarily based on the fact that i got  like a full scholarship that's really  gonna make the biggest difference like  you saving money here and there isn't  gonna make that much of a difference oh  i also worked as a  assistant at the department of  architecture  um so i was helping them like organize  events and  figure out like t